            <ArticleTitle>A snapshot of children's postoperative tonsillectomy outcomes at home.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>186-95</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe home outcomes of pain, medication use, fluid intake, and emesis 24 hr after tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy. The theory of unpleasant symptoms provided a conceptualizing framework.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A secondary analysis was conducted. Outcomes for 76 children were recorded on a home diary that included: self-reported pain, analgesic use, fluids ingested, and emesis. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Children reported moderate pain. Children who received analgesics received less than recommended standards. Only 11% of the children drank the recommended fluids, and 31% of the children vomited at home.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Parents need to become partners in pain management. Recommend multimodal discharge teaching and follow-up phone call at home to ensure adequate analgesic administration and fluid intake.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
